// @check
// @error-at 21:26 a `flat` type cannot be generic
// @error-at 22:26 a `flat` type cannot have a field named 'size'
// @error-at 23:32 `flat type Cycle` is recursive
// @error-at 24:31 `flat type Loop` is recursive
// @error-at 25:28 `flat type Also` is recursive
// The CHECKER-tier declaration-shape rejects (webcraft P1.2). The parser-tier one
// (a non-record body) is `flat-non-record-body-rejected.vl`.
//
//   - GENERIC: a type parameter has no width, and there is no instantiation to
//     measure at a declaration. This is the thing standing between here and a
//     generic `buf.rows<T>`, filed in `flat-records-design.md` §6.1.
//   - `size` FIELD: `N.size` is the record's total byte size, so a field of that
//     name makes the spelling ambiguous. Rejected rather than silently resolved
//     either way — "field wins" hides the record's size and "size wins" hides a
//     field's offset, and both are wrong answers in address arithmetic.
//   - RECURSIVE: a flat record is stored INLINE, so a cycle has no finite size.
//     Found by a fixpoint that STOPS MAKING PROGRESS and reported by name, rather
//     than hung on. The inverted control (`flat-shape-rejects-control.vl`) makes
//     the real claim visible: the same recursion is LEGAL without `flat`, because
//     a non-flat record field is a reference. This is not "recursion is bad".
flat type Gen<T> = { a: T }
flat type Named = { size: i32 }
flat type Cycle = { other: Loop }
flat type Loop = { back: Cycle }
flat type Also = { me: Also }
